       The GNU C library does not employ the ggeetthhoossttnnaammee()  system  call;  in‐
       stead, it implements ggeetthhoossttnnaammee() as a library function that calls uunn‐‐
       aammee(2)  and  copies  up  to _s_i_z_e bytes from the returned _n_o_d_e_n_a_m_e field
       into _n_a_m_e.  Having performed the copy, the function then checks if  the
       length of the _n_o_d_e_n_a_m_e was greater than or equal to _s_i_z_e, and if it is,
       then  the  function  returns -1 with _e_r_r_n_o set to EENNAAMMEETTOOOOLLOONNGG; in this
       case, a terminating null byte is not included in the returned _n_a_m_e.

